# What this package may never name, anywhere under lib/ and test/.
#
# THE TEST FOR A WORD: could a vendor with the same tool and a completely different product still
# use this package with that word in it? A name the tool gives itself passes and stays off this
# list — this package may say method, header, body, status, bearer and field, because those are the
# protocol's own words. A name of one product's interfaces, layout or components fails and goes on
# it, and so does every second tool a request could be pointed at: this package holds the
# conversation and knows nothing about who is on the other end of it.
#
# One word per line. Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored; everything else is matched
# case-insensitively and word-anchored, so a longer name that merely contains a word is no
# occurrence of it.
#
# WORD-ANCHORED CUTS BOTH WAYS, and the blocks below are written knowing it. Anything that is not a
# letter or a digit ends a word, so a bare name reports every longer one built on it with a
# separator: `letsencrypt` reports `letsencrypt-prod` and `LETSENCRYPT_EMAIL` alike, which is why
# only the bare word stands here. A longer name built WITHOUT a separator is a different word and
# has to stand in its own right — `digita` does not report `digitacloud`.
#
# THE LIST LIVES HERE, UNDER tool/, AND NOT BESIDE THE CHECK THAT READS IT. The scan reads test/ to
# the byte, so a list written into test/checks/tool_purity_test.dart would itself be an occurrence
# of every word on it and the check could never pass. That tool/ stays out of the scan is asserted
# by a counter-probe of its own.

# The product this package was cut out of, the platform it stands on, and the organisation behind
# both. This block stands on every list in this tree, word for word.
hostyour
digita
digitacloud
digitaplatform
simetrix
simetrixch

# That product's choices: the distribution it runs on, the components it deploys, the tools it
# drives those components with, the vendors it rents from, and the authority it gets its
# certificates from. Each is a fact of one product, not of the protocol. This block stands on every
# list in this tree, word for word — HTTP itself is this package's tool, so no line of this kind is
# missing here.
microk8s
argocd
tekton
zot
authentik
external-secrets
vault
helm
kubectl
snap
netplan
cloudflare
hetzner
letsencrypt

# That product's own domain words: what it onboards, what it runs things for, and its own word for
# the branch every other branch is cut from. A conversation held by this package may be ABOUT one
# of these — in a program file, which is data — and this package may not know it.
tenant
consumer
trunk
manager
onboard
approve
mint
redeem
